,"E6","F1","T2","T4","T5b","T5e","T9"],"criteria":["E6","F1","COH"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.3875,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["AC2: 'The module passes under synthetic CPU load that previously reproduced the failure.'","Context: 'The module passed 3 of 3 standalone runs right after, at the same load'","No load recipe, run count, or pre-fix RED step is described anywhere"],"finding":"AC2 refers to a 'synthetic CPU load that previously reproduced the failure', but the plan never defines that load. The Context says the module did NOT fail when rerun standalone at the same load, so no reproducer is established. AC2 also gives no proving command (load generator, level, duration, pytest invocation) and no run count. A one-off green run cannot show that an intermittent timeout is fixed.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"ffa4db3dd2096f7c9","impact":0.55,"is_primary":null,"location":"Acceptance Criteria, AC2 vs Context / Problem","norm_id":"n7ff5c043027a3506","priority":0.4125,"reason":"default-advisory","recalled":false,"scenarios":["The executor runs the module once under some load, it passes both before and after the fix, and AC2 is ticked without proving anything","No RED baseline exists, so there is no evidence the chosen load ever exercised the 1s cap on the recovery path"],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Example: start CPU hogs with `for i in $(seq $((2*$(sysctl -n hw.ncpu)))); do yes >/dev/null & done`. Then run `for i in $(seq 20); do pytest -q tests/unit/test_snapshot_submodules_watchdog_c605.py || echo FAIL; done | grep -c FAIL`. Require at least 1 before the fix and exactly 0 after it, and record both counts on the ticket. If the failure cannot be reproduced, demonstrate the fix deterministically instead: e.g. wrap real `git ls-tree` with a ~1.5s sleep on the recovery path and assert the call succeeds.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.75,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"na","